hey this is pressure Locker a standard  dice has the numbers 1 to 6  each appearing with the probability of  1/6 if the dice is modified so the  number 6 appears with half the frequency  of the other numbers what is the new  probability of rolling a 6 I want to  mention I came across this problem in a  new book power up by Matthew Lane the  book uses video games to discuss really  interesting mathematical concepts and I  learned quite a few new things I did  receive a free advanced copy of the book  for review and it's really a great book  you should check it out there's a link  in the video description so returning to  the problem can you figure it out give  this problem a try and when you're ready  keep watching the video for the solution  in a standard dice the probability of  rolling the six is equal to 1/6 which is  the same as rolling any other number in  this loaded dice we want the probability  of six equal 1/2 the probability of  rolling any other number so one way you  might think about solving the problem is  that you might simply decrease the  probability in half that is you would  say the probability of rolling a six  will be 1/2 times 1/6 which equals 1/12  but there's a problem when you do this  if you add up all the other sides which  still have a probability of 1/6 then the  total probability will be too small it  will not add up to 1 you will actually  get that the probability of these  numbers will be equal to 11 over 12 so  this is the wrong answer because we need  the sum of all the probabilities to be  equal to 1  but no worries there's a way to fix this  since we need the probability to be 1 we  can scale each term by 12 over 11  this is so that we have 11 over 12 being  equal to one  once we scale it and if we scale all the  other terms by the same factor  we'll get the correct probability so 12  over 11 times 1 over 12 is equal to 1  over 11 and 12 over 11 times 1 over 6 is  equal to 2 over 11 and when we sum these  new probabilities we do in fact get that  the total probability is equal to 1 so  this is the correct answer the  probability of rolling six will be equal  to one over eleven but the lesson here  is that you can't simply adjust one  probability if you decrease the  probability of rolling a six you have to  compensate by adjusting all the other  probabilities there's another way you  could have come up with this answer  let's suppose P is equal to the  probability of rolling a six we then  have the probability of rolling any  other number will be equal to two P now  we need the total probability of all the  rolls to be equal to 1 so we can  algebraically add up these variables we  have one term of P and five terms of 2p  to be equal to 1 this will be a total of  11 P that's equal to 1 which means P is  equal to 1 over 11 so we get to the same  answer but it was done in an algebraic  way rather than scaling up the  probabilities and adjusting there's  another way you could have solved this  problem you could have also thought  about the odds of the rolls or the  relative probabilities so the odds of  rolling a six will be two to one against  rolling a six it'll be twice as likely  to roll any other number than rolling  the six now in order to convert these  odds  into probabilities what we do is we add  up all of these numbers we have one for  the six and we have five terms of two  this will be equal to 11 in order to  convert these odds into probabilities we  divide each of the relative odds by 11  so we then get to the same answer that  the probability of rolling a six is  equal to one divided by 11  did you figure out this problem and  which method did you use thanks for  watching this video please subscribe to  my channel I make videos on math and  game theory you can catch me on my blog  mind your decisions that you can follow  on Facebook Google+ and patreon you can  catch around social media at pressure  Walker and if you like this video please  check out my books there are links in  the video description
 

